Let $\mathcal{H}_6$ denote a 6-dimensional complex Hilbert space. A pair of quantum orthogonal Latin squares of order 6 is an arrangement of $36$ normalized vectors $\{|\psi_{i,j}\rangle\}_{i,j=1}^{6} \subset \mathcal{H}_6 \otimes \mathcal{H}_6$ into a $6\times 6$ array such that:

(i) the $36$ vectors form an orthonormal basis of $\mathcal{H}_6\otimes\mathcal{H}_6$;

(ii) for every row $i$, the symmetric superposition $\frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\sum_{j=1}^{6}|\psi_{i,j}\rangle$ is a maximally entangled state of the bipartition $\mathcal{H}_6\otimes\mathcal{H}_6$ (i.e., its reduced density matrix on either factor is $\tfrac{1}{6}\mathbb{I}_6$);

(iii) for every column $j$, the symmetric superposition $\frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\sum_{i=1}^{6}|\psi_{i,j}\rangle$ is also maximally entangled.

Decide whether such a pair of quantum orthogonal Latin squares of order 6 exists. (Equivalently, decide whether there exists an absolutely maximally entangled state of four six-level subsystems—a state in $\mathcal{H}_6^{\otimes 4}$ whose reduced density matrix on every two-subsystem marginal is maximally mixed—or equivalently a $((4,1,3))_6$ pure quantum error-correcting code, a 2-unitary matrix in $U(36)$, or a perfect tensor with four indices each ranging over $\{1,\dots,6\}$.)

